We parameterize the MeanFlow predictor 𝒎  m{m} using a neural network, denoted as 𝒎 θ  m{m}_{	heta} , to approximate the true 𝒎  m{m} . For time-dependent PDEs, the network is designed to map the concatenated input [ 𝒖 ⁡ ( 𝒙 , τ ) , τ , 𝒙 , t , 𝒂 ] [ m{u}( m{x},	au),	au, m{x},t, m{a}] , where τ 	au and t t are randomly sampled time pairs during training. The model outputs the average integral from time τ 	au to t t . According to the fundamental identity (Eq. ( 7 )), the target state at time t t is explicitly constructed as 𝒖 ⁡ ( 𝒙 , t ) = 𝒖 ⁡ ( 𝒙 , τ ) + ( t − τ ) ⋅ 𝒎 θ  m{u}( m{x},t)= m{u}( m{x},	au)+(t-	au)\cdot m{m}_{	heta} . The total objective function is defined as the weighted sum of the data loss (measuring the discrepancy between the predicted solution at time t t and observational data) and the physics-informed decoupled spatio-temporal MeanFlow losses: ℒ Total = ℒ Data + λ t ​ ℒ T-MeanFlow + λ s ​ ℒ S-MeanFlow , 	extstyle\mathcal{L}_{	extrm{Total}}=\mathcal{L}_{	extrm{Data}}+\lambda_{t}\mathcal{L}_{	extrm{T-MeanFlow}}+\lambda_{s}\mathcal{L}_{	extrm{S-MeanFlow}}, (11) where λ t \lambda_{t} and λ s \lambda_{s} are the weighting coefficients. Conversely, when γ = 0 \gamma=0 , the system degenerates into a static PDE. In this scenario, temporal variables are omitted. The model instead takes the analytically extended boundary conditions 𝒖 0 ​ ( 𝒙 )  m{u}_{0}( m{x}) (where 𝒖 0 | ∂ D = 𝒖 | ∂ D  m{u}_{0}vert_{\partial D}= m{u}vert_{\partial D} ) and the coefficient field 𝒂  m{a} as inputs. The solution over the interior domain 𝒙 ∈ D ∖ ∂ D  m{x}\in D\setminus\partial D is constructed as 𝒖 ⁡ ( 𝒙 ) = 𝒖 0 ​ ( 𝒙 ) + 𝒎 θ  m{u}( m{x})= m{u}_{0}( m{x})+ m{m}_{	heta} , where 𝒎 θ  m{m}_{	heta} represents the network output. The temporal loss naturally vanishes, and the training objective simplifies to: ℒ Total = ℒ Data + λ s ​ ℒ S-MeanFlow . \mathcal{L}_{	extrm{Total}}=\mathcal{L}_{	extrm{Data}}+\lambda_{s}\mathcal{L}_{	extrm{S-MeanFlow}}. Alg. 1 detail the training opt