k. Selected region and variables. We extract a local spatial block from the HYCOM grid using latitude indices [ 1100 , 1260 ) [1100,1260) and longitude indices [ 2900 , 3060 ) [2900,3060) . This corresponds approximately to latitudes from − 36.0 ∘ -36.0^{\circ} to − 29.64 ∘ -29.64^{\circ} and longitudes from 232.0 ∘ 232.0^{\circ} to 244.72 ∘ 244.72^{\circ} in the HYCOM convention, i.e., approximately 128.0 ∘ 128.0^{\circ} W to 115.28 ∘ 115.28^{\circ} W. The selected depth levels are 0 , 2 , 4 , 6 , 8 , 10 , 12 , 15 0,\ 2,\ 4,\ 6,\ 8,\ 10,\ 12,\ 15 meters. The raw extracted block has shape 32 × 8 × 160 × 160 , 32	imes 8	imes 160	imes 160, corresponding to time, depth, latitude, and longitude dimensions, respectively. Preprocessing. We normalize the extracted temperature field using the mean and standard deviation computed over the selected raw block. The statistics used in our processed dataset are μ = 23.01293796527723 , σ = 1.3310423861925285 . \mu=23.01293796527723,\hskip 18.49988pt\sigma=1.3310423861925285. The raw temperature values in the selected block range approximately from 18.8700 ​ to ​ 26.1490 . 18.8700	ext{ to }26.1490. After normalization, we construct local spatio-temporal samples using a sliding-window procedure. Each sample contains 9 time steps on a spatial grid of size 32 × 32 × 8 . 32	imes 32	imes 8. The spatial stride is set to 4 along the two horizontal dimensions. This produces a total of 26136 26136 samples, each with shape 9 × 32 × 32 × 8 . 9	imes 32	imes 32	imes 8. Dataset split. We split the processed samples into training, validation, and test sets using an 80/10/10 split: 20908 ​ training samples , 2614 ​ validation samples , 2614 ​ test samples . 20908	ext{ training samples},\hskip 9.24994pt2614	ext{ validation samples},\hskip 9.24994pt2614	ext{ test samples}. All methods are trained and evaluated on the same split. Prediction task. Given the initial three-dimensional temperature field and the spatio-temporal coordinates, the model predicts the future temperature evolution over the 9-step temporal window. Unlike idealized synthetic PDE data, the HYCOM data are not generated from a single clean closed-form PDE with perfectly specified boundary conditions. Instead, they are data-assimilative ocean fields influenced by numerical model dynamics, observations, and assimilation procedures. This makes the benchmark more representative of real deployment settings, where data may be noisy, partially observed, and only approximately consistent with simplified PDE assumptions. F.3 Implementation Details of Sec. 4.1 Table 4: Hyperparameter settings for synthetic PDE benchmarks. Dataset Backbone Batch size Learning rate Epochs Loss weights Burgers Conditioned FNO1d 8 5.5 × 10 − 4 5.5	imes 10^{-4} 200 λ t = λ x = 10 − 2 \lambda_{t}=\lambda_{x}=10^{-2} Navier–Stokes Fourier U-Net 5 2.0 × 10 − 4 2.0	imes 10^{-4} 100 λ t = λ x = 10 − 2 \lambda_{t}=\lambda_{x}=10^{-2} Darcy Fourier U-Net 4 3.0 × 10 − 4 3.0	imes 10^{-4} 300 λ x = 10 − 2 \lambda_{x}=10^{-2} Poisson Fourier U-Net 4 3.0 × 10 − 4 3.0	imes 10^{-4} 300 λ x = 10 − 2 \lambda_{x}=10^{-2} For all PDEs, four finite-interval pairs ( τ , t ) ({	au},t) are randomly sampled at each iteration to construct the MeanFlow regularization. For steady-state PDEs, the model takes the coefficient/source field together with the prescribed boundary condition as input. Models are trained with a supervised relative L 2 L_{2} loss and the proposed MeanFlow regularization. All results are reported using the checkpoint with the lowest validation relative L 2 L_{2} error. For Burgers’ equation, we use a conditioned FNO1d. The model takes the current solution state and spatial coordinate as input, and is conditioned on the interval information. It uses Fourier modes [ 12 , 12 , 12 , 12 , 12 ] [12,12,12,12,12] , channel width 96 96 , fully connected hidden dimension 192 192 , GELU activation, and a conditioning MLP with hidden dimension 224 224 . For Navier–Stokes, Darcy flow, and Po